THIS PATCH NO LONGER EDITS `docs/automation/pending/README.md`, AND THAT IS
DELIBERATE. It used to, with a hunk replacing the whole *Waiting now* table by
"Nothing -- the normal state described above", written when this was the only
parked patch in the directory. It is not any more, and a hunk that deletes a
table as one contiguous block breaks the moment anybody adds a row to it -- as
#177 did, and as #173 will. `git apply` tolerates an offset and never an
insertion inside the preimage, and it is all-or-nothing, so a stale row would
have taken the three workflow edits down with it.

So the README edit is an instruction here rather than a hunk, which is the form
that survives a table nobody can stop editing.

TO APPLY:

    git checkout main && git pull
    git apply docs/automation/pending/75-approval-stall.patch
    git rm docs/automation/pending/75-approval-stall.patch
    #  This file only. `git rm -r docs/automation/pending` would also delete
    #  every other parked patch and the README three links in
    #  APPROVAL_STALLS.md point at.

THEN, IN THE SAME COMMIT:

    docs/automation/pending/README.md
                         delete this patch's row from *Waiting now* and the
                         "Verified before it was parked" paragraph about it.
                         Leave every other row alone -- the table is shared. If
                         the table ends up with no rows at all, then and only
                         then say "Nothing -- the normal state described above".
    docs/automation/APPROVAL_STALLS.md
                         the sentences saying the `approvals` job is written and
                         not deployed.

    bash .github/tests/merge-candidate-test.sh   # asserts every patch left in
                                                 # pending/ still applies

    git commit -am "Land the approval-stall watchdog from a local session"

WHAT IT CHANGES, below.

diff --git a/.github/workflows/agent-queue-watchdog.yml b/.github/workflows/agent-queue-watchdog.yml
index b5cbdb3..92df5a5 100644
--- a/.github/workflows/agent-queue-watchdog.yml
+++ b/.github/workflows/agent-queue-watchdog.yml
@@ -299,6 +299,350 @@ jobs:
             fi
           done < /tmp/stranded
 
+  approvals:
+    name: Anything waiting on an approval
+    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
+    if: vars.CLAUDE_AUTOMATION_ENABLED != 'false'
+    # Bounded because this loops over every open pull request and asks the API
+    # about each. Nothing here should take minutes; the number is what stands
+    # between "should not" and a job that runs until the six-hour ceiling.
+    timeout-minutes: 10
+    permissions:
+      # pull-requests: write to comment. Deliberately NOT actions: write --
+      # this job says a run is stalled, it does not re-run it. Option E in
+      # docs/automation/APPROVAL_STALLS.md, and it stays unwritten until
+      # somebody has observed whether a GITHUB_TOKEN re-run clears an approval
+      # requirement that a GITHUB_TOKEN push created. A retry loop that does not
+      # clear it is an hourly bill for the same answer.
+      pull-requests: write
+      actions: read
+      contents: read
+    steps:
+      # Its own job for the same reason `stranded` is: a failure in here must
+      # not cost the tick its actual dispatch, which is the thing with a turn
+      # limit and a bill behind it.
+      - name: Fetch the decision from the default branch
+        uses: actions/checkout@v7
+        with:
+          ref: ${{ github.event.repository.default_branch }}
+          sparse-checkout: .github/scripts
+          sparse-checkout-cone-mode: false
+          persist-credentials: false
+
+      - name: Say so when a check is waiting for an approval nobody asked for
+        env:
+          GH_TOKEN: ${{ github.token }}
+          REPO: ${{ github.repository }}
+        run: |
+          set -euo pipefail
+
+          # A run that completed `action_required` with zero jobs put NO check
+          # on the pull request at all. Everything downstream reads that as "not
+          # failing": the orchestrator merges once CI is green, the merge
+          # sweep's conditions are checks on a verdict that does not exist, and
+          # `agent:review` says the work is with the reviewers. The agent's own
+          # last word is "waiting on CI", which is true and stays true forever.
+          #
+          # This job's whole contribution is that the last word is no longer
+          # that. It fixes nothing -- see APPROVAL_STALLS.md for what would.
+          # `sort=created&direction=asc` is load-bearing, not tidiness. The
+          # REST default here is `created` DESCENDING, and the cap below stops
+          # after three comments -- so the default order serves the three
+          # NEWEST stalled heads every tick and the one that has been stalled
+          # silently the longest is served last. Under sustained agent pushing
+          # that tail never comes up at all, which is #75's own failure mode at
+          # a slower cadence. Oldest first drains it. Found in review.
+          gh api "repos/$REPO/pulls?state=open&sort=created&direction=asc&per_page=100" --paginate --slurp \
+            | jq 'if (length > 0 and (.[0] | type) == "array") then add else . end' > /tmp/pulls.json
+
+          jq -r '.[] | "\(.number) \(.head.sha) \(.head.repo.full_name // "")"' /tmp/pulls.json > /tmp/heads
+
+          # Three per tick, the same bound `pr-merge-sweep.yml` puts on itself
+          # and for the same reason. Without it the FIRST tick after this lands
+          # comments on every open pull request with a stalled head at once --
+          # by this patch's own diagnosis, most agent-pushed heads here -- and
+          # `approval-stall-decision-test.sh` says what that costs: "a guard
+          # that comments on every run is read once and ignored afterwards".
+          # Each comment also bumps `updated_at`, which is the T-147 hazard
+          # below, so the debut is exactly when it is worst. Nothing is lost by
+          # waiting: the marker is per head, the schedule is hourly, and a head
+          # not reached this tick is reached on the next one. Found in review.
+          COMMENTS=0
+          COMMENT_CAP=3
+          # AND A BOUND ON THE INPUT, because the cap above bounds only the
+          # output. Per tick this job spends one `actions/runs` call and one
+          # `issues/{n}/comments` call per open pull request, plus one `jobs`
+          # call per run on each head -- roughly four per pull request here --
+          # against a 1,000/hour `GITHUB_TOKEN` budget shared with
+          # `pr-merge-sweep.yml`, which runs twice an hour. At the ~30 open
+          # pull requests this repository carries that is ~120 calls hourly and
+          # nowhere near the limit; at 250 it would be, and exhausting the
+          # budget produces exactly the failure this job is least able to
+          # report -- `jobs` calls start failing, every head reads unreadable,
+          # and the guard's own call volume is what silenced it. The cap is set
+          # well above the working set so it does not bite in normal operation,
+          # and what it drops is ANNOUNCED rather than silently truncated.
+          # Ascending order means the pull requests it drops are the newest,
+          # which is the right end to drop: the oldest stall is the one nobody
+          # is coming back to on their own. Found in the fifth review round.
+          HEADS_SEEN=0
+          HEADS_CAP=40
+
+          while read -r PR SHA HEADREPO; do
+            if [ -z "$PR" ] || [ -z "$SHA" ]; then
+              continue
+            fi
+
+            if [ "$COMMENTS" -ge "$COMMENT_CAP" ]; then
+              echo "::notice::Reached the $COMMENT_CAP-comment cap for this tick; the rest are left for the next one."
+              break
+            fi
+
+            HEADS_SEEN=$((HEADS_SEEN + 1))
+            if [ "$HEADS_SEEN" -gt "$HEADS_CAP" ]; then
+              echo "::notice::Looked at $HEADS_CAP pull requests this tick, which is the API-call bound; the newest ones beyond that were not examined and are left for the next tick."
+              break
+            fi
+
+            # A fork's pull request genuinely CAN require approval by policy,
+            # and that approval is a decision somebody made on purpose. Saying
+            # "nobody is watching for this" about a deliberate gate is how a
+            # guard teaches people to stop reading it.
+            if [ -n "$HEADREPO" ] && [ "$HEADREPO" != "$REPO" ]; then
+              continue
+            fi
+
+            # The newest run of each workflow for this exact head. `max_by(.id)`
+            # rather than `.run_number`, because run_number is per workflow and
+            # comparing it across workflows is meaningless -- group_by makes
+            # that safe either way, and id is monotonic without depending on it.
+            #
+            # The API returns the LATEST ATTEMPT of each run, which is what
+            # makes this self-healing: once somebody re-runs a stalled run, the
+            # object here reports the re-run's conclusion and this job goes
+            # quiet on its own.
+            RUNS=""
+            if ! RUNS=$(gh api "repos/$REPO/actions/runs?head_sha=$SHA&per_page=100&exclude_pull_requests=true" \
+                          --paginate --slurp </dev/null 2>/dev/null \
+                        | jq -r 'if type == "array" then [.[].workflow_runs[]?] else [.workflow_runs[]?] end
+                                 | group_by(.workflow_id) | map(max_by(.id))
+                                 | .[] | [(.id|tostring), .status, (.conclusion // ""), .head_sha, (.name // "")] | join("|")' 2>/dev/null); then
+              echo "::warning::Could not read the runs for #$PR at $SHA; skipping it this tick rather than guessing in either direction."
+              continue
+            fi
+            [ -n "$RUNS" ] || continue
+
+            # What this pull request has already been told, and about which
+            # commit. Read once per pull request rather than once per run: the
+            # bound is per head, so several stalled runs on one head are one
+            # comment naming all of them, not one comment each.
+            # A read failure here is NOT "never told". The marker is the only
+            # input that bounds output, so resolving an unreadable endpoint to
+            # the empty string would make a 502 or a secondary rate limit read
+            # as "say it again" -- hourly, on the same head. That is the shape
+            # of #82, which this file's own reasoning says it is avoiding.
+            # Skip the pull request instead and try on the next tick.
+            SAID=""
+            RAW=""
+            if ! RAW=$(gh api "repos/$REPO/issues/$PR/comments?per_page=100" --paginate --slurp </dev/null 2>/dev/null); then
+              echo "::warning::#$PR: could not read comments; skipping this tick rather than risking a repeat."
+              continue
+            fi
+            BODIES=$(printf '%s' "$RAW" \
+                       | jq -r 'if (length > 0 and (.[0] | type) == "array") then add else . end | .[].body' 2>/dev/null) || BODIES=""
+            SAID=$(printf '%s' "$BODIES" \
+                     | sed -n 's/.*<!-- attadipa-approval-stall \([0-9a-fA-F]\{7,40\}\) -->.*/\1/p' \
+                     | tail -1) || SAID=""
+            # The PARTIAL marker, written when a job count on this head could
+            # not be read. It deliberately does NOT feed the repeat bound in
+            # the decision script -- the head stays eligible for a complete
+            # report the moment the counts read -- it bounds the INCOMPLETE
+            # report to one. The `-partial` suffix means the pattern above
+            # cannot match it: `-->` after the SHA is required, and the partial
+            # marker's name differs before the SHA, so the two never alias.
+            SAID_PARTIAL=$(printf '%s' "$BODIES" \
+                     | sed -n 's/.*<!-- attadipa-approval-stall-partial \([0-9a-fA-F]\{7,40\}\) -->.*/\1/p' \
+                     | tail -1) || SAID_PARTIAL=""
+
+            STALLED=""
+            GATED=""
+            # Set when ANY run on this head came back with a job count we could
+            # not read. The comment may still go out -- a stall we can see is
+            # worth saying -- but the marker is withheld, because the marker is
+            # per HEAD while the job count is per RUN. Burning the head on a
+            # partial picture answers `repeat` forever for a stall never
+            # mentioned, which is the silence this job exists to break.
+            UNREADABLE=""
+            # A PIPE, not a tab, and the reason is not style. Tab is IFS
+            # *whitespace*, so bash collapses a run of tabs into one delimiter:
+            # a record whose `conclusion` is null arrives a field short and
+            # every later field shifts left. `1<TAB>action_required<TAB><TAB>
+            # 488be1e...<TAB>CI` reads back as CONCLUSION=488be1e...,
+            # RUN_SHA=CI, RUN_NAME="" -- and the decision script then rejects
+            # `CI` as a non-hexadecimal head and returns `quiet|unreadable`.
+            # That kills the `action_required`-as-a-STATUS branch, which is the
+            # one arriving with an empty conclusion, which is the branch this
+            # whole guard argues hardest for. One-directional: after the shift
+            # field 3 is always a SHA, so no false positive is reachable -- only
+            # the missed stall, which is the failure mode with no symptom.
+            # `|` is not IFS whitespace, so empty fields survive it; the idiom
+            # is already used twice in this repository (`failure-reason.sh`,
+            # `setup-labels.sh`). RUN_NAME is last on purpose: it is the only
+            # field that could contain a `|`, and `read` gives the last variable
+            # the unsplit remainder. Found in review, before anyone applied
+            # this; .github/tests/approval-stall-decision-test.sh now runs this
+            # exact line, lifted out of this file, against a null-conclusion
+            # record.
+            while IFS='|' read -r RUN_ID STATUS CONCLUSION RUN_SHA RUN_NAME; do
+              [ -n "$RUN_ID" ] || continue
+
+              # Asked for every run rather than only the suspicious ones, so
+              # that the shape test lives in ONE place. Re-deriving "is this the
+              # approval shape" in shell to save an API call would put half the
+              # decision back in a `run:` block where nothing can execute it --
+              # which is how every defect the hand-over step ever shipped got
+              # in.
+              # `2>/dev/null` keeps the loop going, and on its own it also
+              # eats the reason: if a secondary rate limit takes out every jobs
+              # call on a head, STALLED and GATED come back empty and the tick
+              # is indistinguishable from one where nothing was stalled. Say so
+              # once per run. It changes no decision -- `unreadable` is still
+              # reached by the decision script, after the shape test -- it only
+              # makes a broken guard distinguishable from a quiet one. Found in
+              # review.
+              JOBS=""
+              JOBS=$(gh api "repos/$REPO/actions/runs/$RUN_ID/jobs?per_page=1" \
+                       --jq '.total_count' </dev/null 2>/dev/null) \
+                || { JOBS=""; echo "::warning::#$PR: could not read the job count for run $RUN_ID; its shape cannot be decided this tick."; }
+
+              # Called ONCE and both lines read off the result. Calling it twice
+              # -- once for the decision, once for the reason -- would let the
+              # two disagree if the inputs ever changed between them, which is a
+              # bug that reads as correct code.
+              # And this one is sharper, because `OUT=""` yields an empty
+              # DECISION -- neither `say` nor `quiet|unreadable` -- so the
+              # UNREADABLE line below does not fire and the run is dropped. The
+              # job then walks every pull request, emits nothing and exits
+              # green: not a wrong answer, NO answer, with no line saying one
+              # could not be got. Reachable by the script being missing, or by
+              # `.github/scripts` falling out of the sparse-checkout pattern
+              # above. Found in review.
+              OUT=""
+              OUT=$(bash .github/scripts/approval-stall-decision.sh \
+                      "$STATUS" "$CONCLUSION" "$JOBS" "$RUN_SHA" "$SAID") \
+                || { OUT=""; echo "::warning::#$PR: approval-stall-decision.sh did not run for $RUN_ID; this run is being skipped without a verdict."; }
+              DECISION=$(printf '%s' "$OUT" | sed -n 1p)
+              REASON=$(printf '%s' "$OUT" | sed -n 2p)
+
+              # Set from the DECISION, not from `[ -n "$JOBS" ]`. An empty job
+              # count on a run that is not the approval shape at all -- an
+              # ordinary green one this loop is about to discard -- is not a
+              # partial picture of anything, and letting it withhold the marker
+              # takes the repeat bound off for a reason unrelated to the stall:
+              # one transient 502 on an unrelated run and the same comment posts
+              # every tick for as long as it lasts. The decision script reaches
+              # `unreadable` only AFTER the shape test, which is exactly the
+              # ordering its own comment argues for, so this is the precise
+              # signal and the earlier `[ -n "$JOBS" ]` test was a proxy for it.
+              # Found in review.
+              [ "$DECISION|$REASON" = "quiet|unreadable" ] && UNREADABLE="yes"
+
+              [ "$DECISION" = "say" ] || continue
+              case "$REASON" in
+                # `$STATUS` and `$CONCLUSION` as read, never a literal. The
+                # stalled shape is `status: action_required` with an EMPTY
+                # conclusion -- that is the whole record this job exists to
+                # surface -- so a hardcoded `conclusion: action_required` sends
+                # a maintainer looking for a field the run does not have, in
+                # the only artifact anybody sees. Found in review.
+                stalled) STALLED="${STALLED}- **${RUN_NAME}** — run \`${RUN_ID}\`, \`status: ${STATUS}\`, \`conclusion: ${CONCLUSION:-(empty)}\`, **0 jobs**"$'\n' ;;
+                gated)   GATED="${GATED}- **${RUN_NAME}** — run \`${RUN_ID}\`, \`status: ${STATUS}\`, \`conclusion: ${CONCLUSION:-(empty)}\`, jobs present"$'\n' ;;
+              esac
+            done <<< "$RUNS"
+
+            [ -n "$STALLED" ] || [ -n "$GATED" ] || continue
+
+            echo "::warning::#$PR at $SHA has a run waiting for an approval; saying so on the pull request."
+
+            # WHICH MARKER, AND WHETHER TO SPEAK AT ALL. In the script with the
+            # rest of the rules and with cases of its own, not re-derived here:
+            # the first version of this decided it inline, wrote no marker at
+            # all on the unreadable path, and posted anyway -- so the body's
+            # own closing line was false, the repeat bound was off, and with
+            # the ascending sort and the three-comment cap three heads in that
+            # state starved every newer one. Found in the fifth review round.
+            MARKER=""
+            MARKER=$(bash .github/scripts/approval-stall-decision.sh --marker \
+                       "$UNREADABLE" "$SAID_PARTIAL" "$SHA") \
+              || { MARKER=""; echo "::warning::#$PR: approval-stall-decision.sh --marker did not run for $SHA; saying nothing about this head this tick."; }
+            # Said HERE rather than inside the block below. The block's stdout
+            # is the comment body, so a `::warning::` written in it has to go
+            # to stderr to stay out of the comment -- and whether the runner
+            # parses workflow commands off stderr at all is not established
+            # anywhere in this repository. Rather than rest a diagnostic on an
+            # unverified behaviour, it is emitted on stdout before the body
+            # starts. Found in the fifth review round of #128.
+            if [ "$MARKER" = "partial" ]; then
+              echo "::warning::#$PR at $SHA: a job count was unreadable; reporting what can be seen, once, and re-checking this head next tick."
+            fi
+            if [ "$MARKER" != "full" ] && [ "$MARKER" != "partial" ]; then
+              [ "$MARKER" = "silent" ] \
+                && echo "::notice::#$PR at $SHA: already reported once on an unreadable job count; not repeating until the counts read." \
+                || echo "::warning::#$PR at $SHA: no marker verdict; saying nothing rather than commenting unbounded."
+              continue
+            fi
+
+            {
+              if [ "$MARKER" = "full" ]; then
+                printf '<!-- attadipa-approval-stall %s -->\n' "$SHA"
+              else
+                printf '<!-- attadipa-approval-stall-partial %s -->\n' "$SHA"
+              fi
+              printf "### A check on \`%s\` is waiting for an approval, and nothing was going to ask for it\n\n" "${SHA:0:7}"
+              if [ -n "$STALLED" ]; then
+                printf '%s\n' "$STALLED"
+                printf "A run that finishes as \`action_required\` with **no jobs** put no check on this pull request at all. Nothing is red, so nothing looks wrong — but the orchestrator merges once CI is green, and there is no verdict here to be green.\n\n"
+                printf "The usual cause is that the last push was authenticated with the built-in \`GITHUB_TOKEN\`: GitHub creates the run in an approval-required state and waits. **Two things have actually been seen to clear one**: a maintainer re-running the workflow — from the run's own page, or the Checks tab of this pull request — and closing and reopening this pull request, which re-queues every run on the head. An *approve and run* button may not be there: GitHub documents that control for a pull request from a public fork of a first-time contributor, which is not this case, and the run reads \`completed\` rather than waiting. \`POST /actions/runs/{id}/approve\` is not a way out for an agent: it answered **403 Resource not accessible by integration** when tried under this repository's app installation. The rule, the evidence and what it would cost to stop this happening are in [APPROVAL_STALLS.md](https://github.com/%s/blob/main/docs/automation/APPROVAL_STALLS.md).\n\n" "$REPO"
+              fi
+              if [ -n "$GATED" ]; then
+                printf '%s\n' "$GATED"
+                printf 'These have jobs, so they are visible above and are waiting on a deployment or environment approval — a different thing, and one somebody configured on purpose.\n\n'
+              fi
+              if [ "$MARKER" = "full" ]; then
+                printf 'This is said once per head commit. Push again and it is re-checked against the new one; re-run the checks and this goes quiet by itself.\n'
+              else
+                printf 'One of the runs on this commit would not report how many jobs it has, so **this list may be incomplete** — what is above was visible, what was not is not here. This partial report is also said once per head commit; the counts are re-read every hour, and if they come back a complete report follows. Push again and it is re-checked against the new commit; re-run the checks and this goes quiet by itself.\n'
+              fi
+            } > /tmp/approval-stall.md
+
+            # AND THIS COMMENT BUMPS `updated_at` ON THE PULL REQUEST.
+            # `repos/$REPO/issues` returns pull requests too, and the `stuck`
+            # job below ages orphaned `agent:working` tasks with
+            # `select(.updated_at < $CUTOFF)`. A pull-request-shaped task that
+            # something comments on every tick therefore never gets older than
+            # the cutoff and is never returned to the queue. The marker above
+            # bounds this to one comment per head, so the interaction is bounded
+            # rather than perpetual -- but `merge-candidate.sh` already ages by
+            # head `committedDate` for exactly this reason, and the `stuck` job
+            # does not. That is **T-147**, filed rather than fixed here: this
+            # patch is already blocked on a `workflows` permission and widening
+            # it to change the orphan sweep would put two unrelated behaviours
+            # behind one owner action. Found in review.
+            #
+            # "The marker above bounds this to one comment per head" was false
+            # on the unreadable path until the fifth review round, which wrote
+            # no marker there and posted anyway. Both markers are written now,
+            # so the bound holds on every path and this paragraph is true as
+            # stated -- at most two comments per head, one incomplete and one
+            # complete, and only when the counts change from unreadable to
+            # readable.
+            if gh pr comment "$PR" --repo "$REPO" --body-file /tmp/approval-stall.md </dev/null; then
+              COMMENTS=$((COMMENTS + 1))
+            else
+              echo "::warning::Could not comment on #$PR; leaving it for the next tick."
+            fi
+          done < /tmp/heads
+
   stuck:
     name: Anything stuck
     runs-on: ubuntu-latest
diff --git a/.github/workflows/ci.yml b/.github/workflows/ci.yml
index a6bc8a4..42ce9be 100644
--- a/.github/workflows/ci.yml
+++ b/.github/workflows/ci.yml
@@ -282,6 +282,17 @@ jobs:
       - name: What the agent decides it did
         run: bash .github/tests/handover-decision-test.sh
 
+      # And whether a check that never ran is noticed at all. A run that
+      # completes `action_required` with zero jobs puts NO check on the pull
+      # request, so every downstream reader -- the orchestrator, the merge
+      # sweep, the agent's own "waiting on CI" -- sees "not failing" and waits
+      # forever. #75. The two runs on #71's head `488be1e` are cases here with
+      # the values the API actually returned, so an edit that stops recognising
+      # them fails rather than going quiet, which is the only failure mode that
+      # matters for a guard whose job is to break a silence.
+      - name: A check that is waiting for an approval nobody asked for
+        run: bash .github/tests/approval-stall-decision-test.sh
+
       # And what may be merged with nobody looking. This is the only rule in the
       # repository that can put a commit in `main` unattended, so it gets the
       # heaviest test: every one of the six conditions asserted in both
diff --git a/.github/workflows/claude-agent.yml b/.github/workflows/claude-agent.yml
index cb216bc..94f8f00 100644
--- a/.github/workflows/claude-agent.yml
+++ b/.github/workflows/claude-agent.yml
@@ -806,10 +806,14 @@ jobs:
       # THE BRANCH, FOR THE CODING WORK. Deliberately whatever the event points
       # at, because that is the code the task is about. What it is NOT any more
       # is where the hand-over reads its own logic from -- see the next step.
+      # This checkout pushes. ATTADIPA_AGENT_TOKEN is preferred so an agent push
+      # does not create approval-stalled zero-job runs under GITHUB_TOKEN (#75).
+      # The fallback preserves today's behaviour until the owner sets it.
       - uses: actions/checkout@v7
         if: steps.claim.outputs.acquired == 'true' && steps.auth.outputs.ok == 'true'
         with:
           fetch-depth: 0
+          token: ${{ secrets.ATTADIPA_AGENT_TOKEN || github.token }}
 
       # THE ORCHESTRATION BUNDLE: THE HAND-OVER'S OWN CODE, FROM main, KEPT OUT
       # OF THE SESSION'S REACH.
diff --git a/docs/automation/APPROVAL_STALLS.md b/docs/automation/APPROVAL_STALLS.md
index a46cd87..ddb3f36 100644
--- a/docs/automation/APPROVAL_STALLS.md
+++ b/docs/automation/APPROVAL_STALLS.md
@@ -288,13 +288,13 @@ left unchecked** — *Contents: Read and write*, *Pull requests: Read and write*
 omitted it.** `ATTADIPA_AGENT_TOKEN` is not a checkout credential: it is the
